a privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL158: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite45†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL159: \nL160: cite46†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite47†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL161: \nL162: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite48†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL163: cite49†About†info.arxiv.org · cite50†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite52†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite53†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite54†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite55†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite56†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L164: \nL165: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.08923v1] DynamiQ: Accelerating Gradient Synchronization using Compressed Multi-hop All-reduce (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08923v1)\nciteturn27681view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.08923v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 9 Feb 2026 (this version), latest version 7 Jul 2026 (cite5†v3 )]\nL9: # Title:DynamiQ: Accelerating Gradient Synchronization using Compressed Multi-hop All-reduce\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Wenchen Han , cite7†Shay Vargaftik , cite8†Michael Mitzenmacher , cite9†Ran Ben Basat L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled DynamiQ: Accelerating Gradient Synchronization using Compressed Multi-hop All-reduce, by Wenchen Han and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Multi-hop all-reduce is the de facto backbone of large model training. As the training scale increases, the network often becomes a bottleneck, motivating reducing the volume of transmitted data. Accordingly, recent systems demonstrated significant acceleration of the training process using gradient quantization. However, these systems are not optimized for multi-hop aggregation, where entries are partially summed multiple times along their aggregation topology.\nL17: > This paper presents DynamiQ, a quantization framework that bridges the gap between quantization best practices and multi-hop aggregation. DynamiQ introduces novel techniques to better represent partial sums, co-designed with a decompress-accumulate-recompress fused kernel to facilitate fast execution.\nL18: > We extended PyTorch DDP to support DynamiQ over NCCL P2P, and across different LLMs, tasks, and scales, we demonstrate consistent improvement of up to 34.2% over the best among state-of-the-art methods such as Omni-Reduce, THC, and emerging standards such as MXFP4, MXFP6, and MXFP8. Further, DynamiQ is the only evaluated method that consistently reaches near-baseline accuracy (e.g., 99.9% of the BF16 baseline) and does so while significantly accelerating the training.\nL19: Comments:  | 18 pages, 18 figures\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)\nL21: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | C.2.1; I.2.11\nL22: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2602.08923 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2602.08923v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.08923†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Wenchen Han [cite15†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 9 Feb 2026 17:25:37 UTC (1,